<section class="section section-pad" id="optimal-paths" style="background:transparent;">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="section-kicker">Optimal path computation</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading">A deterministic engine beneath the governance layer.</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">
      Admissibility comes first: a path competes only if it satisfies the governance function (<em
        >P</em
      > ⊨ Φ) — scope, policy, propagation, and cryptographic floor. That filtering is the contribution.
      Only then, as a subordinate step, is the cheapest route selected among the already-admissible candidates
      by weighted shortest-path optimisation.
    </p>

    <div class="optimal-grid">
      <div class="formula-card">
        <span
          style="font-size:0.7rem;letter-spacing:0.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--text-muted);"
          >Objective function</span
        >
        <div class="formula-display">
          <em style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;">P</em>* = <em>argmin</em><sub
            style="font-size:0.62em;vertical-align:-0.4em;"><em>P</em> ⊨ Φ</sub
          > Σ<sub style="font-size:0.62em;vertical-align:-0.4em;"><em>e</em> ∈ <em>P</em></sub>
          <em>w</em>(<em>e</em>)
        </div>
        <p>
          Governance admissibility defines the candidate set; among those paths, the chosen route is
          simply the one with minimum accumulated edge cost.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="optimal-cards">
        <article class="optimal-card">
          <h3>Governance-first admissibility</h3>
          <p>
            The search space is defined by <em>P</em> ⊨ Φ before any cost is compared: non-compliant relations
            never compete. Dijkstra is the current implementation of the optimisation step — an interchangeable
            detail; the model is agnostic to the concrete algorithm.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="optimal-card">
          <h3>Multi-criteria scoring</h3>
          <p>
            Edge weights can combine cryptographic profile, algorithm family, key strength, policy
            compatibility, and structural distance into a single comparable score.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="optimal-card">
          <h3>Explainable trust paths</h3>
          <p>
            Because each path is evaluated through explicit edge costs, the selected chain can be
            justified in terms of governance, cryptographic quality, and structural efficiency.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

